swer":"harmful","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E12","E2"]},{"itemId":"I23","answer":"sufficient","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E8","E9","E10","E14"]}],"evidenceRequests":["Q3","Q1","Q2"],"summary":"The on-time decline is mainly explained by two schedule and construction effects, not absenteeism. The Halvorsen and Bridgeport work zones doubled running times on the corridor routes (E9), and the recovery cut on Group B routes without construction shows a step-down with late first-timepoint departures (E8, E10). The absence rise is largely a recording artifact: ShiftLine began logging partial-day absences on 2026-03-30, full-day absences rose only 1.1%, and missed trips stayed flat (E13-E15). OTP is validated by manual ride checks (E12). The proposed attendance program counting every occurrence would punish partial-day absences caused partly by short layovers, so it is harmful. Restoring recovery time, setting corridor running times, and restating the absence trend are preferred. Suspending OTP publication is harmful."}}